    <div class="post-container" id="fpc-2075281">
                      
		<div class="post" id="post-2075281">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,2075281)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-2075281">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser deleted" data-id="1361261"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/common--images/avatars/default/a16.png" alt=""/>(account deleted)</span> <span class="odate time_1406247210 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">25 Jul 2014 00:13</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2075281">
									

<p>Tooth horror ought to be a subgenre of body horror at this point. Something about stuff to do with teeth is pretty innately creepy, and this piece operates with a good understanding of that dynamic. In a lesser article, the pictures would have seemed like cheap shock value, but paired with the narrative they really help lend a good sense of immersion to what you're doing.</p>
<p>This also has some nice moves to it. The idea of being unable to eat because you're full of <em>teeth</em> is certainly horrifying enough, but I found the cause of death being systemic, bodily <em>tooth decay</em> to be both clever and nightmarish (those of us that have had a lot of work done are probably cursed with an ability to envision something like that). So you've got originality of body horror going for you, which is always appreciated.</p>
<p>I do have some issues. Like Faraday Cage, I'm not terribly fond of the tooth fairies angle. It detracts from the musty, institutional Eastern Bloc science feel that I love so well, and I find the concept of this dude cutting up fairies to be kind of silly in comparison to the rest of the article. I also could have done without the clearly delineated motivation behind this. Making this about a rich dentist with rich clients who want real teeth gave this a little too much mundanity for what it was, and personally I would have found the recovered logs of a Russian scientist obsessed with teeth for some mysterious reason far creepier in this case. And you walk the line a bit with the experiments; the description of the teenage girl at the end came awfully close to exploitation by my reckoning.</p>
<p>In the end, the craftsmanship that went into this and the solid core concept won the day. This is something that is pretty genuinely horrifying, given extra creepiness by the convincing scientific tone. Good work.</p>

							</div>

								
